L000974	\sec{Worldline Quantum Field Theory}
L000975	The two (non-spinning) BHs or NSs are modelled as point particles with trajectories
L000976	$x_{i}^{\mu}(\tau)$.
L000977	
L000978	In proper time gauge $\dot x_{i}^{2}=1$,
L000979	\begin{align}\label{eq:action}
L000980	S=-\sum_{i=1}^{2}\frac{m_i}2\int\!\d\tau\,g_{\mu\nu}\dot x_i^{\mu}\dot x_i^{\nu}
L000981	 - \frac{1}{16\pi G}\int\!\d^{D}x \sqrt{-g} R \, ,
L000982	\end{align}
L000983	suppressing a gauge-fixing term $S_{\text{gf}}$.
L000984	We use a mostly minus signature, dimensional regularisation with $D=4-2\epsilon$ and consider an expansion around the Minkowskian ($G^{0}$) background and straight-line trajectories:
L000985	\begin{align}\label{backgroundexp}
L000986	    x_i^\mu(\tau) = b_i^\mu \!+\! v_i^\mu \tau \!+\! z_i^\mu(\tau)\,,\,\,
L000987	    g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} +\sqrt{32\pi G} h_\mn \,,
L000988	\end{align}
L000989	with the  worldline deflections $z_i^\mu(\tau)$ and graviton field $h_\mn(x)$.
L000990	The initial data consists of the impact parameter $b^{\mu}=b_{2}^{\mu}-b_{1}^{\mu}$ and initial velocities $v^\mu_{1},v^\mu_{2}$, with $b\cdot v_i=0$, $v_i^2=1$ and $\gamma=v_1\cdot v_2=(1-v^{2})^{-1/2}$. We also introduce the parameter $x\in[0,1]$ defined via $\gamma=(x+x^{-1})/2$ to rationalise expressions.
L000991	
L000992	In the WQFT formalism, solutions to the classical equations of motion for the trajectory and metric are given by their tree-level one-point functions,
L000993	$\vev{x^{\mu}_{i}(\tau)}$ and $\vev{g_{\mu\nu}(x)}$.
L000994	Nevertheless, non-trivial Feynman loop integrals arise due to the hybrid nature of the WQFT:
L000995	worldlines conserve only a single momentum component, as opposed to full $D$-dimensional momentum conservation in the bulk.
L000996	The impulse of the first BH, $\Delta p_{1}^{\mu}$, emerges from $\Delta p_{1}^{\mu} = \lim_{\omega\to 0}\omega^{2}\vev{z_{1}^{\mu}(\omega)}$ in momentum (energy) space.
L000997	At 5PM order, the impulse factorises into SF sectors:
L000998	\begin{align}\label{eq:sfExpansion}
L000999	\Delta p^{(5) \mu}_1=  m_{1} m_{2} & \Bigl ( m_2^{4} \Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\text{0SF}}
L001000	+  m_1 m_2^3 \Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\text{1SF}}   \\
L001001	+m_1^2 m_2^2 &\Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\text{2SF}}
L001002	+ m_1^3 m_2 \Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\widebar{\text{1SF}}}
L001003	 + m_1^4 \Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\widebar{\text{0SF}}}\Bigr) \nn \,.
L001004	\end{align}
L001005	In this Letter we compute the unknown even-in-velocity conservative part of $\Delta p^{(5) \mu}_{\text{2SF}}$, while all other parts are known \cite{Driesse:2024xad,Driesse:2024feo}.
L001006	On a basis of $b^\mu$, $v_1^\mu$ and $v_2^\mu$ in the scattering plane, we compute the $b$-component directly ---
L001007	$v_i$-components are reconstructed using the on-shell conditions $p_i^2=(p_i+\Delta p_i)^2$ and momentum conservation $\Delta p_1^\mu=-\Delta p_2^\mu$.
L001008	
L001009	
L001010	\sec{Integrand}
L001011	Generation of the WQFT integrand employs recursive diagrammatic techniques~\cite{Jakobsen:2022fcj, Jakobsen:2022psy, Jakobsen:2022zsx, Jakobsen:2023oow, Jakobsen:2023ndj, Jakobsen:2023hig, Driesse:2024xad, Driesse:2024feo},
L001012	producing a total of {651} Feynman diagrams.
L001013	At the 5PM order, we insert up to six-graviton vertices, whose complexity
L001014	is reduced
L001015	through an optimised non-linear De Donder gauge~\cite{Driesse:2024feo}.
L001016	A subtle point is how to define a \emph{conservative} sector of the truly dissipative
L001017	(in-in) problem in which $\Delta p_{1}^{\mu} + \Delta p_{2}^{\mu}\neq 0$ due to momentum
L001018	(and angular momentum) being carried away by gravitational radiation.
L001019	This {has previously been} achieved \cite{Kalin:2020fhe, Kalin:2020lmz, Dlapa:2021vgp, Jakobsen:2022fcj, Jakobsen:2023ndj, Jakobsen:2023pvx, Driesse:2024xad} by employing Feynman propagators  (in-out) for the gravitons
L001020	and retarded propagators on the worldline (in-in) \cite{Jakobsen:2022psy, Kalin:2022hph}, taking the part real and even in velocity $v$ in the end.
L001021	{In this work we take a different approach, discussed as part of our boundary fixing ---
L001022	for now, we assume only that graviton propagators are individually time-symmetric.}
L001023	
L001024	
L001025	
L001026	
L001027	Our diagrams organise themselves into 14 top-level sectors with respect to the integration-by-parts (IBP) identities, displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams} --- these are indicative of the Feynman diagrams encountered at 2SF.
L001028	A vital difference between 1SF and 2SF is that we cannot use partial fraction identities on worldline propagators to ``planarise'' the entire integrand~\cite{Driesse:2024xad}.
L001029	Nevertheless, partial fractions are still used to resolve linear identities between worldline propagators.
L001030	We require four separate integral families {(P, NP1, NP2, NPM)}, explicit expressions for which are provided in the Supplemental Material.
L001031	The planar family (P) accounts for top-sectors (a--g) of Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams},
L001032	{and all diagrams take a ``Mondrian'' structure without lines crossing each other.
L001033	The first two nonplanar families (NP1, NP2)} capture the possibility of one
L001034	(h--i) or two (j) crossings of the graviton propagators.
L001035	
L001036	{The last non-planar family (NPM),}
L001037	associated with top-sectors (k--m), captures the effect of \emph{non-linear memory}~\cite{PhysRevD.46.4304, Porto:2024cwd, Cunningham:2024dog, Georgoudis:2025vkk}.
L001038	Non-planarity of these diagrams is carried by a vertex where three \emph{active} gravitons (highlighted in red) meet.
L001039	The effect is to produce non-linear corrections to the Einstein equation expanded around a Schwarzschild background.
L001040	There is an $S_{3}$ permutation symmetry about this vertex, which we exploit next to
L001041	shifts on the loop momenta in order to maximally simplify our integrand as preparation for IBP reduction.
L001042	
L001043	
L001044	\sec{Integration-by-parts reduction}
L001045	{The first step towards full integration was finding linear
L001046	IBP identities between the elements of our four integral families,
L001047	enabling reduction to a much smaller basis of master integrals.}
L001048	Our IBP reduction was performed using
L001049	{\tt KIRA 3.0}~\cite{Maierhofer:2017gsa,Klappert:2020nbg, Lange:2021edb, Lange:2025fba}.
L001050	This was the computational bottleneck of the calculation,
L001051	consuming $\sim3\times10^6$ core hours on a high-performance cluster ---
L001052	an order of magnitude larger than 1SF~\cite{Driesse:2024feo, Driesse:2024xad}.
L001053	Yet this number masks the true complexity of the 2SF problem,
L001054	which required two key additional technical improvements:
L001055	\begin{enumerate}
L001056	  \item The use of symmetry relations in the IBP reduction specific to the conservative setup led to a reduction in the number of master integrals. For the { P, NP1, and NP2 families}, we let {\tt KIRA} generate these symmetry relations automatically.
L001057	  Symmetries of the {NPM} family were generated by hand and given as extra equations.
L001058	  \item A careful choice of basis sped up the reduction by an order of magnitude, thereby reducing the polynomial degree of the result.
L001059	  This ``pre-canonical" basis is chosen as close to the canonical integrals as possible without introducing any algebraic or transcendental functions.
L001060	  When using finite-field reconstruction with {\tt FireFly}~\cite{Klappert:2019emp, Klappert:2020aqs}, we observed a drastic reduction not only in the number of probes per prime field but also in the number of prime fields.
L001061	\end{enumerate}
L001062	In summary, we find the following numbers of master integrals (MIs) in our four families
L001063	\be
L001064	\begin{tabular}{l|c|c|c|c}
L001065	Family & P & NP1 & NP2 & NPM \cr
L001066	\hline
L001067	\# MIs & 321 & 144 & 46 & 220
L001068	\end{tabular}
L001069	\ee
