L001103	
L001104	
L001105	To compute a fully canonical system, we follow a similar strategy as outlined in our 5PM-1SF computation \cite{Driesse:2024feo}. The increase in complexity of DEs compared with 1SF leads to increased block sizes up to $16\times 16$, c.f.~Fig.~\ref{fig-2}.
L001106	For the smaller polylogarithmic diagonal blocks, we employ \texttt{CANONICA}~\cite{Meyer:2017joq}.
L001107	For larger sectors, we use the Baikov representation of our integrals \cite{Frellesvig:2024ymq} to find a single integral of uniform transcendental weight (UT). Then we use \texttt{INITIAL}~\cite{Dlapa:2020cwj} to transform the whole diagonal block to canonical form.
L001108	To transform the off-diagonal blocks,
L001109	we use in-house code based on \texttt{FiniteFlow}~\cite{Peraro:2019svx}
L001110	and \texttt{MultivariateApart}~\cite{Heller:2021qkz}.
L001111	For the couplings between polylogarithmic sectors and the CY sectors, we employ integrand analysis using the Baikov representation~\cite{Frellesvig:2024ymq} and IBP-based searches for near-canonical integrals. Using successive transformations, these integrals are rotated into canonical form.
L001112	
L001113	Interestingly, after IBP reduction we find that
L001114	only the K3 sector contributes to our final result.
L001115	The $\mathcal{L}_\text{K3}$ operator (\ref{eq:pfk3}),
L001116	and therefore the integrals in this sector, have a singular point at $x=3-2 \sqrt{2}$,
L001117	or equivalently $\gamma=3$, $v=\sqrt{8}/3$, which lies in the physical region $x \in [0, 1]$.
L001118	We also needed to introduce the square root $r(x)=\sqrt{-1+34x^2-x^4}$ in the canonicalisation process, which shares the same singularity structure. Singularities at $\gamma=3$ appear in partial results but must cancel in the observables.
L001119	
L001120	
