L001123	\sec{Expansion by regions and divergences}
L001124	After $\epsilon$-factorization we construct the solution for the master integrals
L001125	in an $\epsilon$ expansion up to boundary integrals. We determine the boundaries in the small velocity limit ($x \rightarrow 1, \gamma\rightarrow1, v\rightarrow 0$) using the  \emph{method of regions} \cite{Beneke:1997zp, Smirnov:2012gma, Becher:2014oda}.
L001126	
L001127	  This split into regions also plays a role in the separation of conservative and dissipative effects.
L001128	  The regions are characterised by scalings of loop momenta:
L001129	\begin{equation}\label{PRregions}
L001130		  \ell^\text{P}=(\ell^0,\Bell)\sim(v,1)\,,  \qquad
L001131	 	  \ell^\text{R}=(\ell^0,\Bell)\sim(v,v) \, .
L001132	\end{equation}
L001133	We call these \emph{potential} (P) or \emph{radiative} (R) scalings respectively.
L001134	Generally, all loop momenta may be potential but only a few may also become radiative ---
L001135	the active gravitons, highlighted in red in Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams}.
L001136	
L001137	{In the first three families (P, NP1 and NP2)} there are in total four propagators $D_{13}, D_{23}, D_{14}, D_{24}$ (defined in  \Eqn{DIJs} of the supplementary material) with active momenta although at most three can be present in the same diagram.
L001138	In this work we include conservative contributions from regions with an even number of radiative gravitons (as was done at lower orders \cite{Kalin:2020fhe,Dlapa:2021vgp,Jakobsen:2022fcj,Jakobsen:2023ndj,Driesse:2024xad}).
L001139	{There are three regions:}
L001140	the potential region PPPP, the \textit{tail} region composed of RRPP, PPRR, RPRP and PRPR and finally the \textit{memory} region composed of RPPR and PRRP which is a novel 2SF feature --- see Fig.~\ref{fig:boundary}.
L001141	We use the names \textit{tail} and \textit{memory} as the corresponding regions include the tail effect~\cite{PhysRevD.37.1410, PhysRevD.46.4304, Porto:2024cwd} and memory effect respectively.
L001142	The two RR regions, tail and memory, are not related by symmetries in contrast to their subregions.
L001143	
L001144	For the {non-planar memory family NPM},
L001145	the momenta of the propagators {$D_5^\text{NPM}, D_6^\text{NPM}, D_7^\text{NPM}$}
L001146	(defined in \eqref{DNPs} of the supplementary material) are active.
L001147	Only two of those three momenta are, however, linearly independent and give rise to two regions even in R --- PP and RR --- from which we include conservative contributions.
L001148	The non-planar RR region is of the memory type (and does not include any tail).
L001149	
L001150	As is known from the 4PM and 5PM-1SF orders, there is an intricate interplay between the various regions in the form of a cancellation of poles in the dimensional regulator $\epsilon$,
L001151	leading to terms $\propto \log v$ in the final finite result.
L001152	Here, at our 5PM-2SF order a crucial novel feature arises:
L001153	the potential region carries not only an $\epsilon$ pole, but also terms that diverge at $\gamma=3$, which stem from the integrals associated with the K3 geometry.
L001154	Such a singularity was also observed in the PN velocity expansion of the potential region in \cite{Bern:2025zno, Bern:2025wyd}, which the authors attributed to the $\gamma=-1$ pole.
L001155	This novel $\gamma=3$ divergence does \emph{not} occur in the tail region, while the memory
L001156	region carries these velocity divergencies as well. Hence, in order to obtain
L001157	a physically meaningful result we need a second intricate interplay between the regions to cancel divergencies.
L001158	
L001159	
