L001422	\sec{Conclusions}
L001423	In this Letter, we have {computed conservative contributions
L001424	to the momentum impulse and scattering angle}
L001425	in the non-spinning gravitational two-body problem
L001426	at {5PM ($G^{5}$) and 2SF} order using WQFT.
L001427	The complexity of this extraordinarily challenging computation
L001428	{was an order of magnitude larger than the 1SF conservative computation~\cite{Driesse:2024xad},}
L001429	and could only be tackled by greatly innovating our integration strategies.
L001430	The emerging function space is richer than {that of the 1SF case
L001431	(which involved only polylogarithms up to weight 3)
L001432	due to the emergence of a K3 period.}
L001433	
L001434	{A crucial discovery of our work was a spurious divergence
L001435	existing at $v/c=\sqrt{8}/3$, i.e.~$\gamma=3$,
L001436	which} needs to cancel between the potential and memory regions.
L001437	Yet the present prescription for extracting conservative data
L001438	using Feynman propagators,
L001439	{previously adopted at 5PM-1SF order~\cite{Driesse:2024xad},}
L001440	\emph{fails} to provide a finite result.
L001441	Enforcing {cancellation} leads to a novel $\gamma$-3 prescription that we propose.
L001442	This situation calls for a {better} definition of the conservative sector in BH scattering, perhaps relying on the operator $\hat{N}=-i\log\hat{S}$~\cite{Damgaard:2021ipf, Damgaard:2023ttc, Kim:2024svw, Alessio:2025flu, Kim:2025gis, Haddad:2025cmw, Brandhuber:2025igz}. Clearly, we should next turn to the fully dissipative 5PM-2SF computation, as here there is an unambiguous understanding of the worldline formalism using retarded propagators throughout \Rcites{Jakobsen:2022psy, Kalin:2022hph}.
