L001974	A conservative prescription for the evaluation of the memory boundary conditions leading to physically sensible results must satisfy the three requirements listed in the main text below Eq.~\eqref{eq:I12}.
L001975	This is the case for the $\gamma$-3 prescription.
L001976	The idea of this prescription is most easily stated for another choice of memory boundary integrals shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:g3pres}, which keep all three radiative propagators.
L001977	Upon IBP reduction, one may eliminate (``pinch'') one of the radiative gravitons (e.g., the middle one), and in this way they are expressed in terms of the two memory graphs $I^{(M)}_1$ and $I^{(M)}_2$.
L001978	The $\gamma$-3 prescription uses retarded propagators for the memory graphs and averages over two situations: All causality points towards the three-graviton interaction or all causality points away from it.
L001979	Crucially, this prescription satisfies equivalent symmetries to a Feynman prescription, including the $S_3$ symmetry of permuting the three subdiagrams joined by the three-graviton interaction.
L001980	
L001981	We note that a region-by-region prescription for picking out conservative effects is currently also necessary at lower PM orders.
L001982	Generally, for example, contributions from the R region are ignored, although the real part of using the Feynman prescription gives a non-zero contribution to the scattering angle.
L001983	Further, at the 5PM-1SF order, the real part of Feynman also did not produce the expected RRR conservative contributions to the scattering angle.
L001984	Interestingly, we note that at the 1SF order, the established real-part-of-Feynman prescription gives equivalent results to an average of using \textit{only} retarded and \textit{only} advanced propagators, analogous to the $\gamma$-3 prescription for the memory.
L001985	
L001986	It is also interesting to note that the self-force approach currently only gives a clear-cut notion of conservative dynamics at the 1SF order, and one may, therefore, not yet seek guidance there.
L001987	Further, the appearances of divergences in the Lorentz factor $\gamma$ pose an interesting challenge to the post-Newtonian approach: How can one make sure to avoid such poles when expanding around $\gamma\approx1$?
L001988	This is clearly not the case for an angle defined only from potential and tail effects.
